    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. We're also seeing patients where the lesions aren't just irregular, but they have this almost \"malicious\" or aggressive quality to them—they seem to creep and spread in these jagged, disproportionate ways that feel very intrusive to the surrounding tissue. Are there specific ICD-10 terms or code branches that deal with this kind of \"encroaching\" or destructive morphological behavior? I want to make sure we're capturing the severity of the tissue disruption correctly.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When the lesions exhibit an aggressive, encroaching quality, you are moving beyond mere morphology and into the realm of pathological behavior, which is where the coding gets more nuanced. You need to look at codes that describe the *process* of the disease, not just the appearance, which is often where the difference between a slow-growing, benign patch and something more invasive lies. If the spread is jagged and disproportionate, you should be looking at codes related to invasion or malignant potential, depending on the biopsy results. These codes are designed to capture the severity of the tissue disruption, which is crucial for proper reimbursement and clinical tracking.",
      "source": "target",
