    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks, that helps clarify the distinction between appearance and behavior. One final thing—we've had a few cases where the lesions present with these really strange, small, clustered protrusions that almost look like little \"knobby\" or \"gnarled\" growths, almost like something shouldn't be growing there at all. They have this very specific, bumpy, almost almost \"creature-like\" unevenness to the surface. When the texture is that peculiar and doesn't fit standard smooth or scaly descriptors, how do we differentiate between simple textural changes and more significant morphological abnormalities in the ICD-10 system?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Those \"knobby\" or \"gnarled\" protrusions sound like they might be best described using terms related to nodularity or verrucous changes, depending on the scale and nature of the growth. If they are truly bizarre and don't fit standard descriptive categories, you need to see if the documentation supports a code for specific types of growths, like papillomas or specific forms of cutaneous dysplasia, rather than just a general texture code. It's about finding the closest *pathological* match for that unevenness, rather than just describing the look. If the clinical picture is truly unique, you must document the unusual morphology extensively in the notes to justify the chosen code, even if it's a less common selection.",
      "source": "target",
